 in favor  of cleaner more flexible and more  efficient continuous manufacturing  processes  now why won't one be interested in  continuous bioprocessing well if you  think about the switch from batch to  continuous processing you can eliminate  the whole tanks that are required in  current batch manufacturing processes if  your product moves continuously from one  unit operation to another in addition  because all of the operations are  performed at the same time the size of  the manufacturing equipment shrinks  dramatically for a continuous process  relative to that for a batch process and  thus the overall Capital cost for a  continuous process can be orders of  magnitude smaller than that for a batch  process  in addition we can get much greater  productivity because all of the  equipment is in use at all times we  don't have to wait for one operation to  finish before the next operation starts  and we can eliminate all of the  non-value-added steps that are often  required in bioprocessing of batch  systems for example the cleaning and  validation before and after each step  there can be greater flexibility for  continuous processing systems allowing  them to be used in multi-product systems  where we can have closed systems with  minimal transfers and better utilization  of the manufacturing Suite  and there's even the potential for  improved product quality typically  products that are produced in a  continuous system will have much shorter  resonance times within the process which  leads to less aggregation and less  product degradation and there's a more  uniform micro environment which leads to  better control over things like  glycosylation patterns and extent of  deamination  now my group has been very actively  involved in looking at opportunities for  the development of continuous  bioprocessing systems and I wanted to  take the time I had today to go over  three short examples  the first is on the final formulation  step by diet filtration the second 