 could  think about doing this sequentially  where we would take our antibody feed  dilute it with a buffer and then  reconcentrated in a membrane module  which would remove the old buffer  components and leave our antibody in the  desired new buffer  now although this works in principle it  does not work in practice because in  order to get a thousand fold reduction  in the concentration of any of the  buffer components that are currently  present with the monoclonal antibody  product  you would need to use a diet filtration  buffer flow rate that was 999 times  larger than the flow rate of your  antibody product that's completely  impractical first it would require way  more buffer than one could ever utilize  as part of a purification process and  secondly  um  reconcentrating the antibody by a factor  of a thousand in a single pass through a  membrane module is currently well beyond  the technology of even the best membrane  modules that are available commercially  so instead what we have decided to do is  to look at a counter current stage die  filtration process that accomplishes the  same thing that is we do our diet  filtration continuously in a single path  through the membrane unit but it  functions in a practical way with  relatively low flow rates of buffer and  relatively low requirements for the  concentration step by taking advantage  of counter current staging  now this is the diagram of the process  in order to see the counter current  staging it's helpful to look at the flow  rate of the diet filtration buffer and  the monoclonal antibody separately so  the diet filtration buffer is added to  the first module on the left  and we then take the permeate from that  module put it into the second module and  then into the third module and thus if  we were to follow the flow of the diet  filtration buffer it goes from left to  right from module 1 2 to 3.  in contrast if we look at the monoclonal  antibody  it's actually added into the third  module the one that's furthest to the  right and the retentate from that module  is cycled back to the second module and  then the retentate that exits the second  module is cycled back to the first  module that's the antibody is moving  from right to left counter current to  the direction of the disulfrasion buffer  this allows us to collect the purified  or Diet filtered antibody product out of  the very first module right where it is  contacted with the diet filtration  buffer  each of the modules allows us to remove  some of the penultimate buffer  components eventually getting to the  desired degree of diet filtration but we  effectively reuse the buffer so that our  overall buffer requirements are kept  quite well  for those of you with a chemical  engineering background this is in many  ways analogous to the operation of mixer  settler systems for staged extraction  where the settler has been replaced by  the membrane module and the mixing  actually occurs in small static mixers  that we use to mix the diet filtration  buffer and the monoclonal antibody  product right at the exit to each of the  membrane modules  the counter current staging as I  mentioned allows us to get high  performance with relatively little  buffer flow in the system  so we use the model system to  demonstrate the capability of this  device in this case we used vitamin B12  as a model in Purity because it's very  easy to detect by a color or metric  assay We performed our diet filtration  with an IGG or antibody feed at a  concentration of 120 grams per liter  that contain 2 800 milligrams per liter  of vitamin B12 and these are the results  for the vitamin B12 concentration in the  diet filtered product that leaves this  three-stage diet filtration process  as you can see the concentration of  vitamin B12 is perfectly stable over a  24-hour process we ran this continuously  without any uh cleaning or regeneration  of the membrane module  the vitamin B12 leaving this system is  about a thousand folds smaller than the  vitamin B12 concentration in the feed  that is we're removing 99.9 percent of  the vitamin B12 in a single pass through  the three-stage system and this actually  requires about 30 percent less buffer  than traditional batch diet filtration  processes for formulation of a  monoclonal antibody product  now we can actually look at the  optimization of this type of counter  current stage die filtration it's really  a trade-off between the number of stages  and the amount of diet filtration buffer  that we use in the process  and what I've shown here is the  predicted values of the fold removal  that is how much of an impurity can we  remove  as a function of the flow rate ratio on  the x-axis for a single stage system n  equals one a two-stage system n equals  two and then a three and a four stage  system a typical Target in a bio process  might be a thousand fold removal of an  impurity that was present in the system  or a buffer component as you can see we  can do this in a three-stage system  using a flow rate ratio of just about  10. if we tried to do this in a  two-stage system we would require about  a 35-fold flow rate Ratio or a factor of  three and a half more buffer and if we  try to do it in a single stage system  the buffer requirements would be so  large that they're well off this axis uh  the symbols on this platter experimental  data we had both a tooth stage and a  three-stage system that we built in our  lab and you can see that the data are an  excellent agreement with the model  calculations allowing us to use this  type of analysis to optimize the design  of a counter current stage diet  filtration process based on the cost of  the membrane modules which is  proportional to the number of stages the  cost of the buffer which is of course  proportional uh to the buffer flow rate  and the targeted degree of impurity  removal  I want to switch gears and talk about  another exam  under current tangential chromatography  most of you are probably familiar with  protein a chromatography which is used  for the purification of essentially all  monoclonal antibody products  in this case one takes a protein a  ligand  covalently links it to the pore  structure of a chromatography resin and  then packs that into a cow  you can then pump your antibody solution  through that column bind the antibody to  the protein a ligand wash away  impurities change the buffer conditions  to elute the product strip away any  residual impurities and then regenerate  the column for a subsequent cycle again  this is an inherently batch process you  can't start the washing step until  you've bound all your product and you  can't start the illusion step until  you've washed away all the impurities  now there have been a number of attempts  to make what I would consider to be  pseudo-continuous chromatography  processes using multiple columns that  are configured so that you switch  between one column and another  I'm not going to discuss those but I'm  going to instead focus on an alternative  approach which we have referred to as  tangential flow chromatography  in this case we take the chromatography  resin which are these large bluish  particles we use protein a ligands that  are bound to those resins but now  instead of packing the resin in a column  we actually use it in the form of a  flowing flurry and we flow that slurry  over a membrane that allows us to filter  out the host cell proteins while  retaining the large resin particles that  have bound the monoclonal antibody  product  in principle we could use the exact same  approach  for recovering the antibody in this case  we would switch the buffer  the antibody would come off of the  chromatography resin particles it could  be filtered through the membrane  and we would recover the purified and  eluded antibody product in the permeate  while the Reds and particles are  retained by the membrane and recycled  back in the system  thus this new process of continuous  countercurrent tangential chromatography  or cctc  exploits the benefits of affinity  binding in a chromatography step  but it uses counter current staging to  improve the performance of The Binding  washing and illusion steps to reduce the  amount of buffer and enhance the yield  of our antibody product  all of the operations are performed on  this flowing slurry  and we use a combination of hollow fiber  membrane modules and static mixers for  the fluid and resin management that is  to give sufficient residence time for  binding and then to be able to remove  host cell proteins in the washing step  in our monoclonal antibody product in  the illusion step  this really functions as essentially a  conveyor belt are resin particles go  through the binding step then the wash  step then the illusion steps stripping  and equilibration here we've shown  actually two wash steps at different uh  conductivities  the resins just cycle continuously  through and then back to the  meanwhile in The Binding and wash steps  any wholesale proteins that go through  the membrane are collected as waste in  the illusion step we of course collect  our purified product where that product  could then be immediately taken to  subsequent steps in the downstream  process  this is truly continuous and steady  state all the resin is in use at all  times with some of the resin in The  Binding step and some in wash some in  illusion and some in stripping and  equilibration  and it's truly its steady state that is  we continuously pull off product in the  illusion step at a constant flow rate  and a constant product constant  now as I mentioned each stage in the  chromatography system is really just a  combination of a static mixer and a  hollow fiber membrane module the static  mixer provides the residence time for  binding and desorption  the hollow fiber membrane module allows  us to completely separate the resin from  the fluid phase allowing us to recover  the resin particles and wash away all of  the host cell proteins  this is a typical diagram of our picture  of what the system might look like we  have a series of these Hollow fiber  membrane modules and static mixers  they're put in a series Arrangement so  that we can do counter current staging  in a way that is very similar to the  staging that I talked about in the die  filtration process  that's shown here in a two-stage system  where each stage has a static mixer and  a hollow fiber membrane module  the resin slurry comes into this system  on the left goes through the static  mixer the Hilo fiber membrane module  it's retained and then it goes into  stage two  in contrast in this case for the  illusion step the illusion buffer is  added into stage two and the permeate  from stage two is recycled back to stage  one and that allows us to collect the  product from that stage  the staging is absolutely essential to  getting high yield of the product this  is a comparison of experimental data and  model calculations for uh the  performance of the illusion step using a  flow rate of the Aleutian buffer to the  slurry flow rate of three if we did this  in a single stage system we could only  recover about 78 percent of 77 percent  of the product uh because too much of  the product would stay with the flowing  slurry as we go up to Stu state two  stages we can get to 94 three stages we  can get to 98 and you could even drive  this higher if you were to add more  stages  this is even more important in the  washing step where we're trying to get  very very high levels of impurity  removal single stage washing would  require simply unacceptable amounts of  wash buffer but as we go to two three or  even four stages we can get  10 000 fold reductions in the impurity  concentrations while still maintaining  reasonable levels of buffer uh usage for  this type of chromatography process  now in order to make this work we need  to be able to operate the membrane units  continuously for an extended period of  time in order to do this we operate  these membrane units below what's known  as they are critical filtrate flux this  is typical data from a flux stepping  experiment that's used to evaluate the  critical flux we stepwise increase the  flux and look at what happens to the  transmembrane pressure at low values of  the flux the pressure remains quite  stable but as we increase the flux we  eventually get to a point where the  pressure first becomes unstable and this  is what we Define as the critical flux  and we choose to operate usually about  10 to 20 percent below that critical  flux to avoid membrane fouling in the  system  this is typical data for the pressure  profile and The Binding step operated  below the critical flux this was for an  eight-hour process you can see the  pressure remains completely stable  there's no fouling of the membrane  modules in addition the maximum pressure  is only about 2 PSI in contrast to the  100 plus PSI that's used in column  chromatography this makes it very easy  to develop fully disposable systems in  which the entire flow path is just  plastic tubing without any of the  stainless steel piping that might be  required for large pressure operation  as I mentioned this process is  continuous this is the profile for the  eluded product uh we start with an  initial equilibration of the system  where we're not generating any product  uh just before the two hour point we add  in our monoclonal antibody feed and you  can see this is the absorbance profile  coming off of the illusion stage it is  completely steady state it's a short  residence time process meaning that  there's less opportunity for protein  degradation or aggregation and it allows  us to use this as part of a fully  continuous  Downstream process  uh this is a comparison of the  performance of of the system used for uh  antibody purification specifically  looking at the host cell protein level  as well as the aggregate concentration  uh in our clarified Harvest for 