 of the particles to bind  to the protein a ligands and thus we  could get much shorter resonance times  in our binding step compared to what one  would achieve uh with 45 Micron  particles and this smaller revenance  time allows us to develop a higher  productivity uh continuous  chromatography system in this case we  were comparing a column with 45 Micron  particles to our continuous system with  45 Micron particles and then our  continuous system with 25 micron  particles  they all provided very similar yields of  antibody and similar reduction in  wholesale proteins but the big  difference is in the productivity and  the productivity of the continuous  chromatography system using the 25  micron particles is twice that in our  continuous system with 45 Micron  particles and about 12 times larger than  that in the column  note that we could not operate our  column with 25 micron particles because  the pressure drop required to flow  through that column would have actually  crushed the particles and made it  impossible to do the chromatography  um I do need to acknowledge some of the  collaborators on the continuous  chromatography system uh this technology  is being developed by chromatan  corporation uh olec shinkaj is the CEO  and president uh we obtain funding from  the NIH the FDA and the Ben Franklin  technology partners and collaborated as  you can see with a number of companies  in particular Eli Lilly Burke regeneron  Fujifilm diaspin and Spectrum Labs in  addition to Pure light uh and as a point  of disclosure I currently serve on the  board of directors for chromatin  uh in the time that I have remaining I  want to go through one more example uh  which is work that my lab is currently  actively pursuing in looking at a  precipitation process as a low-cost  alternative to protein a chromatography  uh for initial product capture  now precipitation has been used in the  past for the purification of plasma  proteins and one of the reasons that it  works well